  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>📈 Results</h2>
    
    <h3>What It Does</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>~2-Minute Generation:</strong> pulls live financials and produces a full DCF report, compared to the hours a manual build would take.</li>
      <li><strong>Cross-Checked Against Public Estimates:</strong> I compared outputs for a handful of large-cap tickers against publicly available analyst estimates as a sanity check — not against a Bloomberg Terminal, which I don't have access to.</li>
      <li><strong>Repeatable:</strong> the same methodology applies consistently across different companies, which makes side-by-side comparisons more meaningful than ad-hoc spreadsheets.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>DCF valuations are inherently sensitive to assumptions (growth rate, WACC, terminal value) — treat any single output as a starting point for further analysis, not a definitive price target.</em></p>

    <h3>What It's Useful For</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Quickly generating a first-pass valuation to sanity-check against the market price.</li>
      <li>Testing how sensitive a valuation is to WACC and growth assumptions via the sensitivity grid.</li>
      <li>Comparing the same metric across a handful of similar companies.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>🛠️ Technical Methodology</h2>
    
    <h3>DCF Modeling Framework</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Data Collection:</strong> Pull 5-year historical financials + current market data.</li>
      <li><strong>WACC Calculation:</strong> Compute cost of equity (CAPM) and cost of debt (interest coverage).</li>
      <li><strong>FCF Projection:</strong> Forecast 5-year cash flows using historical growth trends.</li>
      <li><strong>Terminal Value:</strong> Apply Gordon Growth Model with conservative GDP growth rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Discounting:</strong> Present-value all cash flows using WACC as discount rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Equity Value:</strong> Subtract net debt to arrive at equity value per share.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Sensitivity Analysis</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Generated 2D grid: WACC (rows) vs Terminal Growth Rate (columns).</li>
      <li>Each cell = intrinsic value under that scenario.</li>
      <li>Color-coded heatmap to visualize valuation range.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Report Generation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Executive Summary:</strong> Current price vs intrinsic value, upside/downside %.</li>
      <li><strong>Financial Overview:</strong> Key metrics (Revenue, EBITDA, FCF) in table format.</li>
      <li><strong>Valuation Details:</strong> WACC breakdown, DCF assumptions, sensitivity table.</li>
      <li><strong>Export Options:</strong> PDF report, Excel model, JSON data for APIs.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>🎓 What I Learned</h2>
    
    <h3>What Worked</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>API Reliability:</strong> the Financial Modeling Prep API was reliable and had solid coverage for large-cap tickers during testing.</li>
      <li><strong>Standardization:</strong> using one consistent methodology across valuations made comparisons more meaningful than one-off spreadsheet models.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Challenges I Ran Into</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Incomplete Data:</strong> some smaller-cap companies had gaps in their reported financials, which needed a fallback for manual input.</li>
      <li><strong>Terminal Value Dominance:</strong> terminal value often makes up the majority of the total valuation, which is a known weakness of DCF models generally — I used conservative growth assumptions to avoid overstating it.</li>
      <li><strong>Negative FCF:</strong> growth companies with negative free cash flow break the standard DCF math, so I added a fallback to relative valuation (P/S multiples) for those cases.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>🚀 Future Enhancements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Monte Carlo Simulation:</strong> Run 10,000 scenarios with random inputs (WACC, growth) to generate valuation distribution.</li>
      <li><strong>Comparable Company Analysis:</strong> Augment DCF with relative valuation (P/E, EV/EBITDA multiples).</li>
      <li><strong>AI Earnings Forecasts:</strong> Use ML to predict future earnings instead of assuming linear growth.</li>
      <li><strong>Multi-Stage DCF:</strong> Allow different growth rates for near-term (5yr), medium-term (5-10yr), and terminal value.</li>
      <li><strong>Bloomberg Integration:</strong> Direct export to Bloomberg Terminal format for institutional clients.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

